{"id":"cholesky-decomposition","t":"Cholesky factorisation, solve and log-determinant","tags":["Linear Algebra"],"d":"Hard","rt":"numpy","fn":"cholesky_solve","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"A symmetric positive definite (SPD) matrix factors as $A = LL^\\top$ with $L$ lower-triangular and $L_{ii} > 0$. It is half the cost of LU, needs no pivoting, and it doubles as a test for positive definiteness: the factorisation succeeds exactly when $A$ is SPD. Gaussian processes and Gaussian log-likelihoods use it for both the solve and the log-determinant.\n\nWrite `cholesky_solve(A, b)` **from scratch** (no `np.linalg.cholesky`, `solve`, `inv`, `det` or SciPy).\n\n## Algorithm\n1. For $j = 0, \\dots, n-1$: compute the pivot $d = A_{jj} - \\sum_{k<j} L_{jk}^2$. If $d \\le 0$, $A$ is not positive definite: **return `None`**. Otherwise $L_{jj} = \\sqrt d$, and for $i > j$: $L_{ij} = \\big(A_{ij} - \\sum_{k<j} L_{ik}L_{jk}\\big)/L_{jj}$.\n2. Forward substitution: solve $Ly = b$.\n3. Back substitution: solve $L^\\top x = y$.\n4. $\\log\\det A = 2\\sum_i \\log L_{ii}$.\n\n## Inputs\n- `A`: symmetric float array `(n, n)`, $n \\ge 1$ (only read the lower triangle).\n- `b`: float array `(n,)` or `(n, k)`.\n\n## Output\n- `None` if a pivot is $\\le 0$.\n- Otherwise a tuple `(L, x, logdet)`: `L` is `(n, n)` with exact zeros above the diagonal, `x` has the shape of `b` and solves $Ax = b$, and `logdet` is a Python float.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef cholesky_solve(A, b):\n    \"\"\"A: SPD (n, n), b: (n,) or (n, k). Return (L, x, logdet) with A = L L^T and A x = b, or None if not PD.\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["Build `L` one column at a time. The pivot for column $j$ only needs row $j$ of `L` to the left of the diagonal; check it before taking the square root.","Forward substitution: `y[i] = (b[i] - L[i, :i] @ y[:i]) / L[i, i]`. Back substitution with $L^\\top$ uses **column** $i$ of `L` below the diagonal: `x[i] = (y[i] - L[i+1:, i] @ x[i+1:]) / L[i, i]`. Both lines work unchanged when `b` is `(n, k)`.","The determinant of a triangular matrix is the product of its diagonal, and $\\det A = (\\det L)^2$, so `logdet = 2 * np.log(np.diag(L)).sum()`. Summing logs never overflows, unlike the product."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef cholesky_solve(A, b):\n    A = np.asarray(A, dtype=float)\n    b = np.asarray(b, dtype=float)\n    n = A.shape[0]\n    L = np.zeros((n, n))\n    for j in range(n):\n        d = A[j, j] - L[j, :j] @ L[j, :j]\n        if d <= 0:\n            return None                                       # not positive definite\n        L[j, j] = np.sqrt(d)\n        # Whole column below the diagonal at once: L[i, j] for all i > j.\n        L[j + 1:, j] = (A[j + 1:, j] - L[j + 1:, :j] @ L[j, :j]) / L[j, j]\n\n    y = np.zeros_like(b)\n    for i in range(n):                                        # L y = b, top to bottom\n        y[i] = (b[i] - L[i, :i] @ y[:i]) / L[i, i]\n    x = np.zeros_like(b)\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):                            # L^T x = y, bottom to top; row i of L^T is column i of L\n        x[i] = (y[i] - L[i + 1:, i] @ x[i + 1:]) / L[i, i]\n    return L, x, float(2.0 * np.log(np.diag(L)).sum())\n","ex_md":"## Why it works\nWrite $A = LL^\\top$ entrywise: $A_{ij} = \\sum_{k \\le j} L_{ik}L_{jk}$ for $i \\ge j$. The $k = j$ term is the only unknown in each equation, so solving for it column by column gives the recursion. The pivot $d$ is the Schur complement of the leading block: it is positive at every step if and only if every leading principal minor is positive, which (Sylvester's criterion) is exactly positive definiteness.\n\nTests 2, 4 and 5 are not PD: $[[1,2],[2,1]]$ has eigenvalue $-1$, $[[1,1],[1,1]]$ is only semi-definite (pivot exactly 0), and $[-1]$ is negative. Test 6 is a classic SPD matrix with integer factor $L = [[2,0,0],[6,1,0],[-8,5,3]]$.\n\n## The log-determinant\n$\\det A = \\prod_i L_{ii}^2$. For the $30\\times 30$ test, $\\det A$ is around $10^{50}$; at a few hundred dimensions the product would overflow. Summing logs is what Gaussian likelihoods use.\n\n## Complexity\n$\\tfrac13 n^3$ flops for the factorisation (half of LU), $O(n^2 k)$ for the two triangular solves.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Taking `np.sqrt` of a negative pivot, which gives `nan` with a warning instead of a clean \"not PD\".\n- Back substitution with `L[i, i+1:]` (row of $L$, all zeros) instead of `L[i+1:, i]` (row of $L^\\top$).\n- Computing `np.log(np.linalg.det(A))`, which overflows to `inf` for large matrices.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- How do you sample from $\\mathcal{N}(\\mu, \\Sigma)$? ($x = \\mu + Lz$ with $z \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, I)$ and $\\Sigma = LL^\\top$.)\n- A covariance matrix fails Cholesky by a hair because of round-off. What do you do? (Add jitter $\\epsilon I$ to the diagonal, the standard fix in GP code.)","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([[4., 2.],\n       [2., 3.]])\narray([2., 1.])","out":"(array([[2.        , 0.        ],\n       [1.        , 1.41421356]]), array([0.5, 0. ]), 2.079441541679836)"},{"in":"array([[1., 2.],\n       [2., 1.]])\narray([1., 1.])","out":"None"}],"lessons":[{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/ml-maths-course/low-rank/quadratic-forms/","t":"Quadratic Forms","c":"ML Maths"},{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/ml-maths-course/linear-systems/the-inverse-matrix/","t":"The Inverse Matrix","c":"ML Maths"},{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/machine-learning/linear-models/kernels/","t":"Kernels","c":"Machine Learning"}],"related":["lu-decomposition","mahalanobis-distance","solve-linear-system"],"ntests":8,"tj":"[{\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[4.0, 2.0], [2.0, 3.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [2.0, 1.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [[2.0, 0.0], [1.0, 1.4142135623730951]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.5, 0.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 2.079441541679836]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 2.0], [2.0, 1.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [1.0, 1.0